```sql
PRAGMA foreign_keys=ON;
CREATE TABLE folders(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY);
CREATE TABLE notes(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, folder_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES folders(id) ON DELETE CASCADE);
INSERT INTO folders VALUES (1),(2);
INSERT INTO notes VALUES (10,1),(11,1),(12,2);
DELETE
FROM folders
WHERE id=1;
SELECT id,folder_id
FROM notes
ORDER BY id;
```
Only note 12 in folder 2 remains. PRAGMA foreign_keys=ON must run before opening a transaction; changing it inside a transaction does not enable enforcement. The child REFERENCES clause names folders(id), and ON DELETE CASCADE states the deletion action. No unrelated folder or note is removed.

```sql
PRAGMA foreign_keys=ON;
CREATE TABLE folders(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY);
CREATE TABLE notes(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, folder_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES folders(id) ON DELETE CASCADE);
INSERT INTO folders VALUES (1),(2);
INSERT INTO notes VALUES (10,1),(11,1),(12,2);
DELETE
FROM folders
WHERE id=2;
SELECT id,folder_id
FROM notes
ORDER BY id;
```
Notes 10 and 11 remain, both in folder 1. Optional practice: predict deleting a folder with no notes. If CASCADE is omitted, our separately validated failed DELETE reports FOREIGN KEY constraint failed and preserves all rows. Current learner checks inspect successful state, not expected-error handling. NO ACTION is not interchangeable with RESTRICT for deferred constraints: RESTRICT acts as soon as the key change occurs even if its constraint is deferred; deferred timing is outside this exercise.

